Today is my birthday, my doctor says I should
no longer watch my favorite football team.
At my bedside lies a list of receipts, and my 
blood pressure pills. Taking aspirin everday 
is like paying rent to live in your own body.
Today a child called me uncle for the first time
and I froze. It made me remember my own uncle.
How he lives so far away now. How we ran 
like wild horses in the acres outside our church
until we believed our bodies were little horses.
How at 16 I measured against him by the basin
and was happy I had grown taller than him, 
but he insisted he would always remain older.
I go back in time, move from memory to memory,
like they are locations. I think I found the proof 
for theoretical physicists that time survives space.
I would love to call him and tell him about the boy, 
about how I grew older than him today. 
